SalesInsight – Development Diary

Week 1: Group Roles and Dataset Exploration

My Role

Data and Backend Developer

Work Completed

* Participated in group discussions to decide each team member’s role and responsibilities.
* Took responsibility for dataset selection and data preparation.
* Searched Kaggle for suitable sales datasets in CSV format.
* Downloaded and compared different datasets based on our project requirements.
* Used Orange Data Mining to explore the datasets, including their columns, data types and missing values.
* Checked whether the datasets contained the required information for sales, product and customer analysis.
* Discussed the available datasets with my group members.
* Helped select the Online Retail dataset for the SalesInsight project.

Challenges

Some datasets did not contain all the information required for our planned analytics features. I also experienced problems importing and displaying a dataset in Orange. I checked the file formats and tested alternative datasets to resolve these issues.

What I Learned

I learned how to search for suitable datasets, compare their features and explore data using Orange. I also understood the importance of selecting a suitable dataset before starting development and having clear responsibilities within the team.

Outcome

Our group finalised the team roles and selected a suitable sales dataset for the SalesInsight project.

Next Steps

* Identify the required columns and data types.
* Plan data cleaning and preprocessing.
* Start developing the data import functionality using Python.


Week 2: Sales Data Import and Preprocessing

My Role

Data and Backend Developer

Work Completed

* Used Python and Pandas in Visual Studio Code to develop the sales data processing functionality.
* Worked on the data pipeline to import CSV sales datasets.
* Created file upload functionality using Streamlit.
* Added validation to check whether required sales columns were available.
* Converted invoice dates, quantities and prices into suitable formats.
* Created additional fields to calculate total sales and analyse daily and monthly performance.
* Prepared sales, product, customer and transaction summaries for the analytics dashboard.
* Added validation checks for the processed dataset.
* Tested the application using valid and invalid CSV files.
* Used GitHub to manage and submit my code for team review.

Challenges

I experienced Python import errors after adding new functions to the data pipeline. I checked the function names, corrected the code and restarted Streamlit. I also encountered a datetime error when processing invoice dates, which I resolved by converting the InvoiceDate column into a proper datetime format.

What I Learned

I improved my understanding of Python, Pandas and Streamlit. I learned how to import datasets, validate columns, convert data types and prepare information for analytics. I also gained experience testing backend functionality and managing code through GitHub.

Outcome

The data import and preprocessing functionality was developed and tested. The processed data could be used for sales calculations, dashboard development and forecasting.

Next Steps

* Improve automatic data cleaning.
* Handle missing values and duplicate records.
* Add a cleaning summary to the application.


Week 3: Automatic Data Cleaning

My Role

Data and Backend Developer

Work Completed

* Developed and improved automatic data cleaning functionality using Python and Pandas.
* Checked the dataset for missing and duplicate records.
* Handled missing product descriptions and country values.
* Removed records with missing essential transaction information.
* Replaced missing customer IDs with a consistent placeholder.
* Removed duplicate sales records.
* Standardised customer IDs and cleaned text columns.
* Added validation checks to confirm that the cleaned dataset was suitable for processing.
* Created a cleaning summary showing original rows, processed rows, removed rows, duplicates removed and missing values handled.
* Connected the cleaning functionality to the Streamlit application.
* Tested the cleaning process and reviewed the displayed results.

Challenges

I faced an error because the cleaning summary expected a missing_values_handled value that was not included in the pipeline statistics. I checked the Python functions, updated the statistics and restarted Streamlit. After troubleshooting, the cleaning summary displayed correctly.

What I Learned

I learned how to handle missing data, remove duplicate records and standardise information using Pandas. I also improved my debugging skills and learned how to display data quality information in a Streamlit application.

Outcome

The automatic data cleaning functionality was successfully tested. The application was able to process uploaded sales data and display a summary of the cleaning results.

Next Steps

* Review the processed dataset with the team.
* Support the integration of cleaned data into the dashboard.
* Continue developing additional sales analytics features.

Week 4: Sales Anomaly Detection

My Role

Data and Backend Developer

Work Completed

* Developed a Sales Anomaly Detection page for the SalesInsight application using Python and Streamlit.
* Used cleaned sales data from the Home page to calculate daily sales totals.
* Implemented a rolling average and standard deviation method to identify unusual increases and decreases in sales.
* Added interactive sliders to adjust the rolling window and anomaly detection threshold.
* Created summary metrics and a sales chart with markers for detected anomalies.
* Added a table to display unusual sales records.
* Saved the anomaly detection Python file in the project’s pages folder.
* Used GitHub Desktop to commit and publish my code.
* Created the mansha-anomaly-detection branch and opened pull request #17 for team review.

Challenges

The VS Code terminal did not recognise the Git command, so I used GitHub Desktop to commit and publish my changes. Initially, I created the anomaly detection file outside the correct folder. I moved it into the pages folder and corrected the project structure. I also experienced a branch comparison issue while creating the pull request, which I resolved by changing the base branch to main.

What I Learned

I learned how rolling averages and standard deviation can be used to detect unusual sales patterns. I also gained experience creating interactive Streamlit pages, organising Python project files, troubleshooting GitHub issues and submitting work through pull requests.

Outcome

The Sales Anomaly Detection feature was developed and uploaded to the mansha-anomaly-detection branch. Pull request #17 was created successfully. Final testing and team review were still pending.

Next Steps

* Test the anomaly detection feature using the project dataset.
* Verify that the chart, summary metrics and detected anomalies display correctly.
* Review team feedback and fix any remaining issues.
* Merge the feature into the main project after approval.
